🔴 3. Insecure Storage of Encrypted Credentials with Co-located Key Material
Risk

Critical

CVSS 3.1

9.1 (Critical)

Vulnerability Category

Broken Cryptography / Sensitive Data Exposure

Description

It was observed that encrypted credential files are stored along with their corresponding key and IV files in the same directory. All required components for decryption are accessible from shared paths. This design nullifies the effectiveness of encryption. No secure key management mechanism is implemented.

Impact
Attackers can decrypt credentials and gain unauthorized access
Leads to full compromise of database and application services
Sensitive data exposure across environments
Recommendations

It is recommended to implement secure key management by storing encryption keys in a centralized vault (e.g., HSM or secrets manager). Separate keys from encrypted data and restrict access using role-based controls. Encrypt sensitive data using strong algorithms and rotate keys periodically. Avoid storing cryptographic materials in shared directories.

References

https://owasp.org/Top10/A02_2021-Cryptographic_Failures/

PoC Steps

Step 1: Locate encrypted password file
Step 2: Identify key and IV files in same path
Step 3: Verify accessibility
Step 4: Confirm decryption feasibility

OWASP Category

A02: Cryptographic Failures

